“A RESOLUTION AUTHORIZING THE MAYOR AND THE CITY CLERK TO SIGN
A FINAL PLAT FOR MAVERIK SUBDIVISION, A REPLAT OF A PORTION OF TRACT 25
AND ALL OF TRACT 26, SUNNYSIDE ADDITION, SECOND FILING, CHEYENNE,
WYOMING (LOCATED AT THE NORTHEAST CORNER OF THE INTERSECTION OF E.
PERSHING BLVD. AND N. COLLEGE DRIVE).”  Albert Simpson, property owner, reviewed the
status of the Maverik store project and subdivision development generally, and requested the final
plat be approved without all of the design elements identified for Maverik as communication received
from Maverik’s representative indicates the company does not intend to proceed with its development
plans.  He stated it was unknown at this time who the future user will be, or the type of development
that may be proposed, for the subdivision. He commented that even though a postponement was
requested at Public Services Committee, since that time he had determined he wished to move
forward with final plat approval.  Mr. Collins moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Pierson. Concerns
were expressed with Planning Commission last minute design (aesthetic) conditions being placed on
the Maverik building project.  Pursuant to Public Services Committee’s recommendation, Mr. Collins
moved to postpone to July 14, 2008, seconded by Mr. Brown.  John Fafoutakis stated his belief that
Maverik should be included in the postponement decision, and Mr. Simpson spoke against
postponement.  Motion to postpone failed.  Voting “no” – all members of the governing body.
Information was provided on Planning Commission conditions and staff recommendations.  Upon
inquiry, T.J. Dlubac, Development Office, suggested a two week postponement to allow staff time
to review implications of an unknown type of development for the site relative to the PlanCheyenne
document.  Mr. Simpson reviewed areas of design elements that had been agreed upon and expressed
disagreement with any further design elements being required.  Discussion included issues involving
```

### Page 5 of 11

```text
design elements with Martin Matsen, Development Director, advising staff recommendations had not
been presented at the last Public Services Committee meeting as a request to postpone had been
received.  He stated staff recommendations were pursuant to an anticipated agreement with Maverik
and conversations with Mr. Simpson concerning plat requirements for property use, other than what
was initially proposed by Maverik, had not been held.  He explained possible circumstances of
approving the plat without staff recommendations relative to Maverik potentially changing its position
and moving forward with its project.  Discussion continued regarding Planning Commission and staff
recommendations, ownership issues being resolved with County Assessor, development agreement
provision, and access easement and bus pull-out area relative to Maverik’s project.  Mr. Matsen
reviewed the right-of-way issue pursuant to the proposed use by Maverik and anticipated
development agreement, and site plan review and approval process.  Comments continued on
concerns with approving the final plat without knowing what will be built on the property and design
elements; approval of other plats without knowledge of specific future use; site plan process assisting
with design elements when a use is identified for the property, and lack of future governing body
approval for any site plan submitted if the plat is approved.  Mr. Segrave moved to amend to remove
staff recommendations #1 through #3 and Planning Commission recommendations #1 and 2,
seconded by Mr. Laybourn.  Mr. Matsen advised if the plat is approved without staff recommendation
#2, the property would be developed pursuant to a community business zone and criteria identified
in PlanCheyenne would not be enforceable to be included in a site plan.  Upon inquiry concerning
when the City’s proposed Unified Development Code (UDC) project would be presented, Mr.
Matsen stated the consultant and staff anticipate submitting the UDC to the governing body this fall.
Mr. Collins raised a point of information on whether staff recommendations and Planning Commission
conditions had been approved at the last City Council meeting, or recommended by Public Services
Committee. Following a 10 minute recess to research past action(s) on the resolution, Mayor Spiker
reconvened the meeting at 7:28 p.m. Upon request, City Clerk Carol Intlekofer reviewed history of
past actions and postponements, and clarified there had been no official vote taken on the staff
recommendations at the last City Council meeting.  She reviewed staff recommendations and Planning
Commission conditions pursuant to resolution information contained in the Clerk’s agenda file.  Mr.
Segrave advised since the recommendations and conditions had not previously been approved, he was
withdrawing his motion to amend.  Mr. Laybourn, as second to the motion, concurred.  Discussion
included other past developments and requirements for certain design and architectural elements, and
economic and financial recession concerns related to current community business developments.  In
response to comment concerning design elements involving development of certain properties,
developer Frank Cole stated he did not agree with comments made regarding a development
involving his business property interest.  Comments continued on communication by governing body
members with the initial project developer to discuss issues of concern, purpose of Council
Committee meetings, and quality community growth and design criteria.  Motion carried. Voting
“yes” - all members of the governing body with the exception of Ms. Broyles, Mr. Collins and Dr.
Rinne voting “no”. (#5031)
