“A RESOLUTION AUTHORIZING THE MAYOR AND THE CITY CLERK TO SIGN
A COUNTY FINAL PLAT AND SUBDIVISION PERMIT FOR FLINT HILL DIVIDE, A
REPLAT OF THE E½ OF TRACT 10, CHEYENNE IRRIGATED GARDENS, LARAMIE
COUNTY, WYOMING (1807 GORDON ROAD).”  Larry Perry, Terrestrial Surveying, agent for
Mr. Flint, property owner, explained the purpose of the final plat is to divide the property for family
residential purposes (additional structure).  Mr. Collins moved to adopt, seconded by Ms. Broyles.
Mr. Collins moved to amend by substitute, seconded by Ms. Broyles.  It was noted a letter had been
received by the County Planning and Development Director advising the original resolution submitted
had been prepared on County letterhead and the Director has requested the document be amended
to remove the letterhead emblems as well as correcting a clerical error by removing reference to the
County’s subdivision permit in the entitlement.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” - all members
of the governing body.  Concerns were expressed with current Gordon Road right-of-way width
relative to potential future annexation and roadway width to meet City requirements and provisions
for an access easement.  Upon inquiry, Abby Yenko, Laramie County Planning office, advised another
plat will be before the governing body in the near future with an additional 10' right-of-way included,
and stated the plat meets current MR-2 zoning district requirements. She reported the Cheyenne-
Laramie County Zoning ordinance states “alleys and/or access easements should not be considered
as primary access” and County staff’s interpretation is that it does not state “shall” and, therefore, it
could not be required on the plat. She indicated the County Attorney had reviewed the matter and
advised the easement should stand as is.  Comments followed on right-of-way width and future
development.  Mr. Collins moved to require an additional 10' of right-of-way for Gordon Road,
seconded by Mr. Brown.  Mr. Perry explained if there is future development or annexation of the
property, it would need to be re-platted to be compliant with urban standards and stated at the
present time the owner is not interested in any other development other than allowing other family
members to reside upon the property.  He stated if the additional 10' right-of-way is required a
revision to the plat will be needed, and suggested a note be placed on the plat reserving the 10' right-
of-way area for future needs. Lisa Pafford, Assistant Director, City Development office, commented
that there is no clear answer on what a road reservation is and what it means to different governing
bodies and instead suggested dedication of the right-of-way be noted (on the plat) for future roadway
use. John Fafoutakis spoke against the amendment and its effect on the property owner pursuant to
their proposed family residential use.  Discussion continued on meeting minimum City roadway width
requirements for potential future annexation/development, and property access issues.  Motion to
amend carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of the governing body.  Upon inquiry, Ms. Pafford
advised staff suggested 30' of suitable street access be provided pursuant to the City-County Zoning
ordinance as it could become problematic for development in the future, and stated the property could
be re-platted and re-configured at a future date. Main motion as twice amended carried.  Voting “yes”
- all members of the governing body.  (#5033)
