CONSIDERATION OF BIDS:
Consideration of Bid #M-28-08 for a contract between the City of Cheyenne and Knife River
for the Missile Drive Overlay Project (2003-2006 1% Sales Tax). John Fafoutakis spoke in favor of
concrete vs. asphalt for the project and commented on other road/street projects involving asphalt
and limited road life; Frank Cole reviewed favorable asphalt overlay results involving his business
```

### Page 10 of 11

```text
properties and spoke generally on asphalt vs. concrete maintenance issues; Victor Ciccone spoke in
favor of spending additional funds to recycle the concrete and properly repair the roadway as it is a
gateway entrance into the City, and Amber Ash stated it would be fiscally responsible to repair the
road sections with concrete and not an asphalt overlay which will need to be replaced later.  During
comments and upon inquiry, City Engineer Ken Lewis confirmed the roadway is being reviewed by
the Metropolitan Transportation Organization and at some future point it is hoped funding will be
allocated to completely reconstruct the roadway, and provided information on current surface and
sub-surface roadway conditions.  Joe Stephenson, Knife River representative, advised the company
has a crew ready to start the project and discussed project cost increases (i.e. fuel) since the firm
submitted its bid and lack of an escalation clause in City bid documents.  He cited his experience with
past asphalt resurfacing projects and durability, and commented on issues affecting roadway life (i.e.
water, weather and freeze/thaw effects).  Mr. Segrave moved to approve in an amount not to exceed
$304,802.50, seconded by Mr. Brown.  Mr. Segrave moved to postpone to May 27, 2008.  Motion
died due to lack of a second.  Discussion followed.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Lewis explained the past 1%
sales and use tax ballot question approved by voters indicated the concrete street would be repaired
and the proposed project will repair the roadway so it is serviceable until a full reconstruction can be
accomplished, and advised any Portland cement concrete curbing that is sound will remain in place
but if some curbs have deteriorated to where it is no longer sufficiently attached to the concrete
pavement, they will be removed and the contractor will install asphalt concrete curbing.  Mr.
Stephenson advised Knife River has an asphalt curb machine.  Upon inquiry, Jeff Fanning,
Engineering Services Director, advised the concrete median under the Burlington Northern Railroad
overpass does not have any bearing on the structural integrity of the roadway and there are no plans
to do anything with the median.  Mr. Lewis provided further information on current roadway
conditions relative to deterioration and “movement” of concrete slabs and discussed aspects of the
overlay project.  Mr. Fanning explained visual site inspections conducted to determine concrete slab
movement, and elements of a “crack and seat” treatment.  Discussion continued on the last grind and
overlay on Dell Range; interpretation of “concrete repair”; placing asphalt over concrete; completely
re-doing the road; industry acceptance of asphalt over concrete; asphalt road life expectancy, and an
overlay as the most cost-effective solution.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of the
governing body with Ms. Case and Mr. Laybourn voting “no”.  (#5244)
